WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.740
Water's polarity gives it three jobs in the cell.

2
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:12.700
First, it sticks to itself through a network of hydrogen bonds, which is cohesion.

3
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:19.540
Second, it dissolves charged and polar molecules, wrapping each ion in a hydration

4
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:25.600
shell. Third, and most important for this chapter, it rejects nonpolar molecules,

5
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.180
and that rejection is what drives the hydrophobic effect.

6
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.460
Water is never just the solvent; it's an active player.

7
00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:40.153
The hydrophobic effect is the exception that runs this chapter; it's driven by

8
00:00:40.153 --> 00:00:41.953
entropy, not by attraction.

9
00:00:42.913 --> 00:00:46.073
The second law says a system trends toward disorder.

10
00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:52.353
When nonpolar molecules sit alone in water, the water is pushed into more ordered,

11
00:00:52.873 --> 00:00:56.773
cage-like arrangements around them, which is low entropy and unfavorable.

12
00:00:58.273 --> 00:01:02.633
Cluster the nonpolar groups together and you release that caged water back into

13
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:04.713
its free, high-entropy state.

14
00:01:05.613 --> 00:01:10.993
The molecules huddle because water gains disorder by pushing them together, and that

15
00:01:10.993 --> 00:01:13.493
drives protein folding and membrane assembly.

16
00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:19.037
Here's a picture for the hydrophobic effect: a crowded dance floor.

17
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:25.257
Scattered wallflowers, isolated nonpolar molecules, force everyone else to

18
00:01:25.257 --> 00:01:30.177
navigate around them, and the surrounding water locks into low-entropy cages.

19
00:01:31.277 --> 00:01:36.317
Push the wallflowers into one corner and the floor opens up, and the dancers, the

20
00:01:36.317 --> 00:01:37.817
water, are free to move.

21
00:01:38.657 --> 00:01:41.657
The force isn't attraction between the wallflowers.

22
00:01:41.977 --> 00:01:45.997
It's the system maximizing its freedom by getting them out of the way.

23
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.900
The same entropy that folds proteins builds your membranes.

24
00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:55.660
A phospholipid has two greasy tails that water won't wet.

25
00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.540
A single-tailed molecule is cone-shaped and packs into a sphere, a micelle.

26
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:07.480
But a bulky two-tailed phospholipid is more cylindrical, so it can't form a tidy

27
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:14.500
sphere; instead it lines up tail to tail into a sheet, and that sheet closes into a

28
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.500
bilayer that seals itself shut.

29
00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.680
Enzymes make the lipids, but no enzyme zips them into a bilayer.

30
00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.940
Water does, by refusing to touch the tails.
